COSTS = {
"furnace-installation": """<p>Furnace prices depend first on the equipment: efficiency rating, single-stage versus two-stage or modulating burners, and a standard or variable-speed blower. Sizing matters too, though bigger isn't better. Installation conditions often make the biggest difference between two quotes: new PVC venting, lining or capping an old chimney, moving the gas line, adding a condensate pump, or modifying plenums and returns. Removal and disposal of the old furnace, the permit or registration where required, and add-ons like a humidifier or thermostat are listed separately.</p>""",
"furnace-repair": """<p>Furnace repair prices depend on what failed and how long it takes to diagnose. Cleaning a flame sensor or clearing a condensate line is a quick, modest job. Igniters, pressure switches and capacitors cost more, and inducer motors, control boards and gas valves more again. Blower motors, especially variable-speed ones, are among the most expensive parts. Parts availability for older or less common furnaces can affect both price and timing. We confirm the price in writing before starting.</p>""",
"heat-pumps": """<p>Heat pump costs depend on capacity, whether the unit is a cold-climate model, and whether it's variable speed. A hybrid system that keeps or adds a gas furnace costs more up front but provides backup heat. Ductwork may need changes because heat pumps move more air. Electrical work, often a new circuit and sometimes a panel upgrade, is done by a licensed electrician and quoted separately. A compatible thermostat, outdoor stand, line set and any available rebates all affect the final number.</p>""",
"boiler-services": """<p>Boiler repair costs depend on the part and access: zone valves and pressure relief valves are modest, while circulators, expansion tanks and control boards cost more. Replacement depends on the boiler type, efficiency and whether it's a combi unit that also heats water. Converting to a high-efficiency boiler often means new venting and a condensate drain. Older systems may need near-boiler piping, air separators or new pumps. Radiant floor work adds mixing valves and zone controls.</p>""",
"ac-installation": """<p>Air conditioner prices depend on capacity, efficiency rating and whether the compressor is single-stage, two-stage or variable speed. A matched indoor coil is part of every proper install, and sometimes the furnace blower or plenum needs work for the new coil to fit. Line sets may be reused after flushing or replaced if they're damaged or the wrong size. Electrical changes, a new disconnect, a pad or wall bracket, and difficult access for the outdoor unit can all add to the total.</p>""",
"ac-repair": """<p>AC repair costs vary with the part and the cause. Capacitors and contactors are among the most common and affordable repairs. Outdoor fan motors, condensate pumps and coil cleaning cost more. Refrigerant leaks take time to locate, then require repair, a pressure test, evacuation and a recharge, and the refrigerant type affects the price. Compressor failure is the most expensive repair and on an older system usually leads to a replacement conversation. We price everything in writing first.</p>""",
"ductless-mini-splits": """<p>Mini-split prices are driven by the number of indoor heads, their style and the size of the outdoor unit. Single-zone systems cost the least; multi-zone systems add a head and line set for each room. Cold-climate heating models cost more than cooling-focused units. Long line set runs, wall or ceiling penetrations, condensate pumps, line set covers and outdoor brackets all affect the total, along with the dedicated electrical circuit, which is quoted separately.</p>""",
"indoor-air-quality": """<p>Air quality upgrades vary widely by product. A media filter cabinet is one of the more affordable improvements. Bypass humidifiers cost less than steam humidifiers, which need their own power and water supply. Whole-home dehumidifiers and HRVs or ERVs cost more and may need dedicated ducting, drains and electrical work. Installation is simplest during a furnace replacement. We recommend only the equipment that addresses your actual problem.</p>""",
"ductwork": """<p>Ductwork pricing depends on access and scope. Sealing accessible joints and adding a balancing damper are modest jobs. Adding a return or a new run means cutting into floors, walls or bulkheads, and the cost rises with finished ceilings and longer distances. Replacing a trunk line or redesigning a section of the system is a larger project. Zoning adds dampers, a control panel and thermostats. We list each change separately so you can prioritize.</p>""",
"duct-cleaning": """<p>Duct cleaning costs depend on the size of the home, the number of supply and return runs and how much buildup there is. Homes with multiple furnaces, very long runs or heavy construction debris take longer. Cleaning the blower wheel and furnace cabinet, extra registers, and access in finished basements also affect time. If we find damaged or disconnected ducts, repairs are quoted separately, never added without your approval.</p>""",
"hvac-maintenance": """<p>Maintenance pricing depends on the equipment being serviced and how many units you have. A single furnace or air conditioner tune-up is the simplest visit. Heat pumps, boilers, and systems with humidifiers, HRVs or UV lights take a bit longer. Maintenance plans bundle a heating and a cooling visit each year, often with extras like priority scheduling. Filters, and any repairs found during the visit, are quoted separately.</p>""",
"water-heaters": """<p>Water heater costs depend on type and capacity. Conventional atmospheric tanks cost the least, power-vented tanks more, and tankless units more again. Venting changes are a common difference between quotes: a new PVC route, a chimney liner or capping an unused flue. Tankless units may need a larger gas line and a dedicated outlet. Expansion tanks, shut-off valves, drain pans and haul-away of the old tank are included or listed in your quote.</p>""",
"smart-thermostats": """<p>Thermostat installation costs depend mainly on the model and the wiring. If a common wire is already available, installation is quick. Running new thermostat cable through finished walls takes more time. Heat pumps, hybrid systems, multi-stage equipment and accessory control need more setup and testing. Remote room sensors and any zoning equipment are additional. We confirm compatibility with your system before you buy.</p>""",
"commercial-hvac": """<p>Commercial HVAC costs depend on the equipment type, tonnage, access and the condition of what's there. Rooftop unit replacements may need a crane, curb adapters, new gas and electrical connections and roof work around the curb. After-hours scheduling to avoid disrupting the business adds labor cost. Maintenance contracts are priced by the number and type of units and the visit frequency. We provide written scopes for owners and property managers to review.</p>""",
}

